  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Sunday...\n\nTemperatures have warmed into the 70s most places this afternoon,\nwith breezy northwest winds over the east (20-40 mph gusts).\nHeading into tonight, a jet max moving overhead and weak warm air\nadvection may bring isolated to widely scattered showers (15-25%)\nto northeastern areas, mainly after midnight into the morning \nhours.\n\nWarm, dry and breezy conditions can be expected for Tuesday, ahead\nof a stronger shortwave and associated cold front that will drop \ninto the region from the north Tuesday night/Wednesday. Winds \nincrease during the morning Tuesday, with westerly winds gusting \nto 40-55 mph over the western foothills during the afternoon, with\ngradually lower winds to the east. Temperatures will warm into \nthe 80s with some areas approaching 90 degrees. While the recent \nrainfall and green up should prevent widespread fire weather \nconcerns, there are areas that have seen less precipitation, \nleading to local elevated fire weather concerns. Take care not to \ncause a spark.\n\nAs the aforementioned wave and associated cold front drop through\nthe region Tuesday night/Wednesday, breezy northwest winds are \nexpected to spread over the entire area through Wednesday. \nWidespread wind gusts of 25 to 40 mph are expected, with the \nstrongest winds over the far east and down along the east side of \nthe Bighorns where gusts of 40-50 mph (moderate chance to reach 50\nmph) are possible. Shower and thunderstorm chances increase \nbehind the front for northern and eastern areas into Wednesday as \nwell (15-50% chance). Increased instability and shear may bring a \nfew stronger storms to northeast MT, while weaker thunderstorms \nare expected down into southeast MT at this time. SPC currently \nhas a marginal risk for severe thunderstorms over NE MT into the \nDakotas. Temperatures will be a good 10-20 degrees cooler on \nWednesday with highs in the 60s and 70s.\n\nNorthwest flow looks to remain over the area through Friday, with\nweak waves producing isolated showers/thunderstorms (20% chance)\nat times, mainly along our periphery. Heading into the weekend,\nensembles continue to prog southwest flow developing over the \nnorthern Rockies as upper troughing moves into the West. This \nwould bring a better potential for daily thunderstorms over the \nupcoming weekend with shortwaves lifting into the region and \nplains moisture advecting into the area. Temperatures are forecast\nin the 70s most days Thursday through Sunday. STP",
